<h2>Why Cabinet Paint Is Not the Same as Wall Paint</h2><p>Wall paint is designed to cover large, flat surfaces and breathe through minor flex. Kitchen cabinets take a completely different kind of abuse: grease, steam, repeated touching, and constant opening and closing. Using standard wall paint on cabinet doors almost always leads to chipping and peeling within a year or two. Cabinet-specific paints are formulated with harder resins that cure into a durable shell rather than a soft film. That difference is what keeps a painted cabinet looking sharp after two years of daily cooking instead of looking worn after six months.</p><h2>Choosing the Right Finish</h2><figure class="blog-figure"><img src="/images/stock/web/blog-how-to-choose-the-right-paint-for-kitchen-cabinets-2.jpg" alt="Kitchen cabinet doors prepped for a new finish" width="1100" height="619" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></figure><p>Finish is the single most important decision after choosing the paint formula itself. Each sheen level has trade-offs between durability and how much surface imperfection it shows.</p><h3>Satin</h3><p>Satin is the most popular choice for kitchen cabinets. It reflects just enough light to feel clean and polished without highlighting every scratch or ding in the wood. It wipes down easily and holds up well to grease and moisture, which matters in a kitchen that sees real cooking year-round.</p><h3>Semi-Gloss</h3><p>Semi-gloss is slightly harder and even easier to clean than satin. The trade-off is that it shows surface flaws, brush marks, and uneven prep work more clearly. If your cabinet boxes and doors are in good shape and your prep is thorough, semi-gloss is an excellent long-term choice.</p><h3>What to Avoid</h3><p>Matte and eggshell finishes are too porous for kitchens. They absorb grease and moisture rather than repelling it, and they do not stand up to regular wiping without wearing through the finish. Save those sheens for bedrooms and living rooms.</p><h2>Water-Based vs. Oil-Based Paint</h2><p>A decade ago, oil-based alkyd paints were the go-to for cabinets because they leveled beautifully and cured rock-hard. The downside was long dry times, strong fumes, and a yellow cast that developed over time on whites and light colors. Today, water-based alkyd hybrids give you most of the hardness and self-leveling of an oil formula with much lower odor and faster recoat times. <a href="https://www.sherwin-williams.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener nofollow">Sherwin-Williams</a> and <a href="https://www.benjaminmoore.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener nofollow">Benjamin Moore</a> both offer water-based cabinet enamels that painters in Western Massachusetts rely on for this reason. For homes built before 1978, it is also worth noting that the EPA regulates how existing paint is disturbed during prep work, particularly if you are sanding old layers before repainting.</p><h2>Color Choices That Hold Up Over Time</h2><p>Trendy colors are tempting, but kitchens are expensive to repaint, so it pays to think a few years ahead. Whites, off-whites, and soft grays remain the most resale-friendly choices and pair well with the wide range of countertop materials common in older New England homes. Deep navy and forest green have also shown staying power and read as classic rather than trendy. Whatever color you choose, go a shade lighter than you think you want. Cabinet paint dries slightly darker than it looks on a chip, and enclosed cabinet surfaces can feel heavier than expected once all four walls of a kitchen come together.</p><p>If your cabinets are a natural wood tone right now, keep in mind that darker woods require a shellac-based or stain-blocking primer before topcoats. Skipping this step allows tannins to bleed through and create a yellow or brownish tint in light paint colors, often within a few months.</p><div class="blog-cta-inline"><p>Would you rather we handled it? Estimates are always free.</p><a class="btn btn--gold" href="/contact.html#estimate-form">Get a Free Estimate</a></div><h2>Prep Work Determines the Final Result</h2><p>Even the best cabinet paint will fail if the surface underneath is not ready. Proper prep includes cleaning every surface with a degreaser to remove cooking oils, light sanding to give the paint mechanical adhesion, and a bonding primer formulated for the cabinet material. For New England homes, humidity is a real factor. Painting during the cold, dry months of a Western Massachusetts winter can actually be ideal for interior cabinet work because lower humidity helps paint cure properly. Avoid painting on days when the house is unusually damp, such as during a stretch of rainy spring weather with windows open.</p><p>Remove all doors and hardware before painting. Painting cabinets in place with doors on leads to drips, uneven coverage along the hinge side, and missed spots in corners. A proper job means taking the doors off, labeling them so they go back in the right place, and painting flat surfaces horizontally to reduce runs.</p><p>If the scope of the project feels large or the existing cabinets have significant damage, <a href="/services.html#cabinet-painting">our cabinet painting service</a> can help you assess whether repainting or a full refinish is the better path.</p><section class="blog-faq"><h2 id="faq">Frequently Asked Questions</h2><h3>How long does painted cabinet finish last?</h3><p>A properly prepped and painted cabinet finish typically lasts five to ten years with normal use. Durability depends heavily on the quality of the paint, the primer used, and how well the surface was cleaned and sanded before painting. High-traffic kitchens may show wear sooner near handles and edges.</p><h3>Do I need to sand cabinets before painting them?</h3><p>Yes, light sanding is necessary to give the primer and paint something to grip. Without it, paint tends to peel away from smooth factory finishes or previously painted surfaces. You do not need to sand down to bare wood, just enough to scuff the surface and remove any gloss.</p><h3>Can I paint over cabinets that were previously painted?</h3><p>Yes, as long as the existing paint is in good condition and not peeling or flaking. Clean the surface thoroughly, sand lightly, and apply a bonding primer before your topcoat. If the old paint is chipping badly, stripping back to bare material will give you a better long-term result.</p><h3>Is it worth hiring a professional for cabinet painting?</h3><p>For most homeowners, yes. Cabinet painting requires careful prep, the right materials, and a controlled application process to avoid brush marks and uneven sheen. A professional result also typically includes a more durable finish than a DIY brush-on job because of spray application and proper curing time between coats.</p></section>
